# Article: Toolset (ruby)

Quality toolset for Ruby / Rails projects — Minitest, Rails-omakase flavored.

These are the project's quality tools. Where a role prompt names a
purpose (coverage, mutation, crap, dry, boundaries, acceptance), use
exactly the command below — no substitutes.

## acceptance

    bin/rails test:system

Rails system tests (Capybara + headless browser) are the acceptance layer — the omakase answer, no Gherkin runner. Slow by design: never fold into the unit lane, and mutation never runs against it.

## boundaries

    bin/packwerk check

Packwerk enforces package boundaries; start as one root package and carve out a package only when a boundary is worth defending. Plain-Ruby gems: a dedicated Minitest boundary test asserting the require graph instead.

## coverage

    COVERAGE=1 bin/rails test

SimpleCov + simplecov-lcov (enabled in test_helper when COVERAGE is set) writes lcov to coverage/lcov.info. Regenerate before reading any coverage-derived metric; stale coverage invalidates everything built on it.

## crap

    crap4rb --lcov coverage/lcov.info <source-dir>

CRAP = complexity^2 x (1-coverage)^3 + complexity, computed per method from source + lcov — same formula and bar as crap4net: every method <= 6 (exit 2 lists offenders). Requires fresh coverage first.

## dry

    jscpd --min-tokens 50 --reporters console <source-dir>

Language-agnostic; same tool and bar as the dotnet toolset. Duplication within the slice's scope gets removed or explicitly justified.

## mutation

    bundle exec mutant run --integration minitest

Scope runs to the slice's subjects (e.g. -- 'Bujo::Sync*'). mutant-minitest maps tests to subjects via a cover declaration in each test class — a new test without `cover` is invisible to mutation. Surviving mutants in the slice's scope are work: kill them with better tests or record precisely why each survivor is unkillable.

## test

    bin/rails test

Full unit suite; green before any handoff. Test stack: Minitest as Rails ships it — plain assertions, fixtures over factories, real objects against the real test database. Doubles only at true external edges (network, clock); Mocha when unavoidable. Do not use RSpec or factory_bot in new tests. rubocop-minitest + rubocop-rails-omakase clean. Plain-Ruby gems (no Rails): bundle exec rake test. Browser/system tests belong under acceptance, never here.
